WEBVTT

NOTE Planned 164-second native capture. Retiming and factual review required
before publication. This file is not evidence of a finished video.

00:00.000 --> 00:08.000
What if two computers cannot be online together?
RelayPost brings back store-and-forward.

00:08.000 --> 00:17.000
Prepare an encrypted parcel, carry it,
and return proof of a verified save.
This is the real native Mac app.

00:17.000 --> 00:26.000
The guided demo creates two disposable identities
and 21 MiB of synthetic files in a new directory.

00:26.000 --> 00:35.000
One machine. A directory carrier.
No physical USB or second computer is demonstrated.

00:35.000 --> 00:43.000
The sender exported a real encrypted, signed parcel:
20 data shards and 4 parity shards in the first group.

00:43.000 --> 00:51.000
The smaller tail has its own budget.
Exported still means waiting for a receipt.

00:51.000 --> 01:02.000
Two data shards and two parity shards are damaged.
Their hashes expose all four erasures.
This group remains repairable.

01:02.000 --> 01:15.000
A separate five-erasure receive attempt is refused:
no committed batch and no success receipt.
The evidence remains; the fifth shard is restored.

01:15.000 --> 01:29.000
The production engine reconstructs ciphertext,
checks its digest, decrypts and validates the files,
then publishes a complete batch.

01:29.000 --> 01:43.000
Four repaired shards. Actual source and received
SHA-256 values match. The receiver has saved the files.
The sender is still waiting.

01:43.000 --> 01:55.000
Now the signed receipt returns. The sender checks
the expected recipient and parcel binding,
then becomes Confirmed.

01:55.000 --> 02:05.000
Carried, repaired, saved and confirmed are separate facts.
A receipt proves this save, not readership or permanence.

02:05.000 --> 02:16.000
CLI and GUI share one Rust demo engine.
The report preserves real inspections,
repaired counts, hashes and states.

02:16.000 --> 02:25.000
UUCP inspired the workflow; NNCP remains a modern baseline.
RelayPost brings the handoff into one native journey.

02:25.000 --> 02:35.000
Actual Kiro CLI specifications and implementation records
are linked in the repository. The initial product used Codex.

02:35.000 --> 02:44.000
Rust builds and tests run in GitHub Actions.
Two-device, physical-media testing and an easier
signed installation remain future work.
